# SYNOPSIS

```text
id:name:leader:members
```

# DESCRIPTION

fsd(4) keeps its users and groups in the file `users` at the root of the volume's `adm` branch, `/adm/users` where that branch is mounted on `/adm`, as Plan 9's file servers keep theirs.
The format is Plan 9's users file, not ndb(6): one line a user, its fields separated by `:`.

`fsd` reads the file when it starts, and again whenever the last open of a file called `users` in the `adm` branch is closed, so an edit takes effect when the editor closes it.
distd(8) reads it through `/adm` to decide who may write its `ctl`, by the same rules, and both read it with `lib/vx-users`.
install(8) writes it when it makes the system volume, and vxfs(8)'s `mkfs` when it makes an image with an `adm` branch; after that it is changed only by writing it through `fsd`.
It is created owned by user 0 and group 0, mode 0664, so the members of group 0 may change it.

Each line defines a user and a group with the same name and id.
The file is split into lines at newlines; a line that is empty or starts with `#` is skipped.
Nothing is trimmed: a blank or a carriage return is part of the field it is in.
The fields are:

: <id>
  The user's number, in decimal, 0 to 4294967295: what a vx-fs entry stores as its `uid`, `gid` and `muid` (see vxfs(6)).
  It must be present and all digits.
: <name>
  The user's name: 1 to 32 bytes, any but `:` and newline.
  It is what a Tattach's `uname` names.
: <leader>
  The group's leader: the name of a user in the file, before or after this line, or nothing.
  A group with no leader is led by each of its members.
: <members>
  The group's members besides the user itself, names of users in the file separated by commas, 32 at most; empty items are skipped.
  It may be empty.

A line with fewer fields leaves the rest empty, so `5:bob` defines a user with no leader and no members.
Everything after the third `:` is <members>, so a further `:` makes a member name no user has, and the file malformed.

A user is a member of its own group as well as of the group of each line that lists it.
Group 0 is the administrators' group, whatever it is called: its members may write `/adm/ctl`, attach permissively and give files away, as fsd(4) describes.

The user called `none` is always present.
If the file does not name it, `fsd` adds it, with id 4294967294.
A Tattach whose `uname` names no user attaches as `none`, which gets only the others' permission bits.

The file is malformed if a line has no <name> or one longer than 32 bytes, an <id> that is not a number of that range, a <leader> or member that is no user in the file, more than 32 members, or if there are more than 127 users or the file is 64 KiB or longer.
A malformed file changes nothing: the users stay as they were, and `fsd` prints `fsd: /adm/users is malformed: the users stay as they were`.
If there is no file it can read when it starts, the users are those of the file

```text
0:adm:adm:
1:none::
```

and it prints `fsd: no /adm/users it can read: adm and none only`.

# EXAMPLES

The file install(8) writes, read from an image vxfs(8) made:

```rc
% vxfs mkfs t.img 64 adm home
% vxfs cat t.img adm /users
0:adm:adm:vectra
1:none::
1000:vectra:vectra:
```

`adm` is user 0, led by itself, with `vectra` a member, so `vectra` administers the volume; `vectra`, user 1000, owns `/home`'s root.

# FILES

: `/adm/users`
